<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Area</title>

  <exercise workspace="2.4in">
    <statement>
      <p>Given the two parabolas <m>y=x^2-5</m> and <m>y=3-x^2</m>,
sketch their graphs and find the area of the region between them.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2.4in">
    <statement>
      <p>Graph the functions <m>f(x) = \cos x</m> and <m>g(x)=\sin x</m> and find the
  area of one of the regions bounded by their graphs.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Find the area between the graphs of <m>y = x^3</m> and <m>y=x</m>.
<em>Over</em></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>The curve <m>x=y^2-12</m> and the line <m>y=x</m> are shown in the
figure below.  I would like for you to find the region between them by
slicing the region into <em>horizontal</em> rectangles as shown.  What is
the width of each rectangle?  What is the height?  How do you then
find the area?</p>
      <p><image source="figures/horizontalarea.eps"/></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose you need to paint the region between the graph
  <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis.  You start painting at <m>x=0</m> and work
  your way to the right.  However, you only have enough paint to cover
  18 square units of area.  Where does your paint run out?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Sometimes we can use area to help us evaluate a definite
  integral.  For instance, the circle of radius 5 centered at the
  origin is described by <m>x^2+y^2 = 25</m>.  Write the upper-half of this
  circle by solving for <m>y</m> in terms of <m>x</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does this tell you about the integral <m>\ds\int_{-5}^5
  \sqrt{25-x^2}~dx</m> or the integral <m>\ds\int_{0}^5
  \sqrt{25-x^2}~dx</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>Apply the same kind of thinking to evaluate the integral
  <m>\ds\int_{-2}^4 |x|~dx</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Sometimes we want to be able to work with situations having an
  unknown parameter in them.  For instance, if <m>a</m> is some unknown
  constant, find the area between the graph <m>y=a-x^2</m> and the
  <m>x</m>-axis.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
